{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 1, :comments ["<b>That shall go before us</b> — they desired many divinities. <b>For this Moses, the man who brought us up from the land of Egypt</b> — he would show us the way by which to ascend; <b>we do not know what has become of him</b> — now we need many divinities that shall go before us. This is the language of Rashi (Rashi on Exodus 32:1). But his formulation is not precise. Rather, this verse is the key to knowing the true nature of the Calf and the intention of its makers, for it is well known that Israel did not suppose that Moses was Elohim, and that by his own power he performed for them the signs and wonders — so what reasoning would lead them to say, since Moses has gone from us, let us make a god? Moreover, they explicitly said \"divinities that shall go before us\" — not that they would attribute to them power to grant life in this world or the world to come. Rather, they were seeking another Moses. They said: Moses, who guided us on the way from Egypt until now — for the journeys were made by the word of YHWH through the hand of Moses (Numbers 9:23) — has been lost from us. Let us make ourselves another Moses who will guide the way before us by the word of YHWH through his hand. This is the reason they mentioned \"Moses the man who brought us up\" — not \"the El who brought us up\" — for they needed a man of Elohim. And so you may understand this from Aaron's response to our master Moses: \"What did this people do to you that you have brought upon it a great sin?\" (Exodus 32:21), and his response was \"And they said to me, 'Make us a god...' And I said to them, 'Whoever has gold, let them strip it off,' and they gave it to me and I threw it into the fire\" (below, verses 23–24). Now Aaron is defending himself before Moses and says to him, \"Let not the anger of my lord burn\" (Exodus 32:22), and this is as though he adds transgression to his sin, for he says that they sought from him idolatry and he made it for them with his own hands — so why should his anger not burn, and what sin is greater than this?\nBut the matter is as I have said: they did not seek the Calf to be for them a god who kills and grants life, and they did not accept the worship of its divinity upon themselves. Rather, they wanted it to be for them in place of Moses, as a guide for their way. And this is Aaron's defense: he claimed that they did not say to me except \"make us divinities that shall go before them in your place, my lord,\" for they did not know what had become of you, and whether you would return or not. Therefore they needed someone to guide their way all the while that you are not with them, and if perhaps you return, they will abandon it and follow after you as at first. And so the matter was, for as soon as the people saw Moses, they immediately abandoned the Calf and spurned it — they let him burn it and cast its dust upon the face of the water, and not one of them objected to him at all. And so you may see that he did not rebuke them and said nothing to them, but when he came into the camp and saw the Calf and the dances, they immediately fled from him, and he took the Calf and burned it and made them drink it, and they did not refuse at all. And had it been a god to them, it is not the way that a person would let his king and his god be burned with fire — even if he burn their abomination before their eyes, they would not stone him.\nNow Aaron was the one who produced this figure, for they did not say to him to make an ox or a lamb or a goat or anything else. And this is the statement of the Sages, who said (Sanhedrin 63a) that it teaches they desired many divinities, for they did not know what to choose and which would be best for them. And Aaron's intention was this: because Israel was in the wilderness of desolation, and desolation and everlasting waste come from the north, as it is written (Jeremiah 1:14), \"From the north shall the evil break forth upon all the inhabitants of the land\" — the intention is not the king of Babylon alone, as is evident from the verse, but rather that from the left side the attribute of judgment always comes to requite all the inhabitants of the earth for their evil. And behold, in the account of the Chariot it says, \"the face of an ox on the left of the four of them\" (Ezekiel 1:10). And therefore Aaron reasoned that the agent of desolation would guide the way through the place of desolation, for there its great power lies, and when they worship there toward the El, a spirit would flow from on high, as was drawn upon Moses. And this is what he said: \"A festival to YHWH tomorrow\" — that the worship and the sacrifices would be for the Name, the unique one, in order to draw favor from Him toward the master of the figure, for with it before them they would direct their intention toward its significance. And our Rabbis taught us this matter — they who revealed its secret — they said (Exodus Rabbah 3:2, 42:5, 43:8): \"I have surely seen the affliction of My people\" — the Holy One, blessed be He, said to Moses: Moses, you see one vision but I see two visions. You see them coming to Sinai and receiving My Torah, but I see how they deliberate concerning Me, that I go forth with My retinues to give them the Torah, as it is written (Psalms 68:18), \"The chariots of Elohim are twice ten thousand, thousands of shin'an,\" and they remove one from My tetramulin, as it is written (Ezekiel 1:10), \"and the face of an ox on the left,\" and they provoke Me with it in the tetramulin — four teams: tetra in Greek means four, as they said (Nazir 8b): tetragon, four. mulin — teams, like the mula'ot of the house of the Master (Shabbat 152a). A parable for the four living creatures that bear the Chariot. And in Vayikra Rabbah (10:3) it says that Aaron said: Since I am building, I build it in the name of the Holy One, blessed be He, as it is written (Exodus 32:5), \"And Aaron called and said, 'A festival to YHWH tomorrow'\" — a festival to the Calf tomorrow is not written here, but rather to YHWH. And Rabbi Elazar said that in this place, the Glory of Elohim rests in the form of a body. And if you set your mind to the first journey (Exodus 14:19), you will understand this. But this is not correct in my eyes, for the Calf was not made through the craft of the wisdom of the constellations so that the Glory would rest in its form; rather, the matter is only that they made the figure in order to direct in its worship toward its significance. And I have already explained (above, 13:21, 14:19) the secret of the first journey, and Heaven forbid that Aaron intended to liken it to that — rather, from it he derived that their journey would be from the direction of that attribute. And the enlightened will understand:"], :book :exodus, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 32, :notes {0 "The text cites Rabbi Elazar (ר\"א) by abbreviation; resolved as Rabbi Elazar based on context and parallel usage."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:20:14.917083503Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}